WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:04.980
A started JVM is still cold.

2
00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:10.841
A fresh JVM ran the same 20,000 card-number checks, ten rounds in a row.

3
00:00:11.033 --> 00:00:13.522
Round one took about 29 ms.

4
00:00:13.522 --> 00:00:17.669
From round six on, each round took 2 to 3 ms.

5
00:00:17.669 --> 00:00:19.789
The code didn't change.

6
00:00:19.789 --> 00:00:20.895
The JVM did.

7
00:00:21.667 --> 00:00:27.098
A fresh JVM starts in the interpreter and counts how often each method runs.

8
00:00:27.098 --> 00:00:30.456
When a method gets hot, C1 compiles it quickly.

9
00:00:30.456 --> 00:00:34.529
If it stays hot, C2 compiles it again, using the profile.

10
00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:36.948
That climb is warm-up.

11
00:00:36.948 --> 00:00:43.164
Every new JVM makes it again, on every pod, after every deploy and every scale-out.

12
00:00:43.967 --> 00:00:48.265
CRaC, Coordinated Restore at Checkpoint, takes a different route.

13
00:00:48.265 --> 00:00:51.439
A checkpoint writes a warm, running JVM to disk.

14
00:00:51.439 --> 00:00:54.547
A restore starts a new process from that image.

15
00:00:54.733 --> 00:00:56.592
On Linux, CRaC uses CRIU.

16
00:00:56.592 --> 00:01:06.629
It freezes the process and writes its memory pages, each thread's CPU registers, and the kernel state it can describe into image files.

17
00:01:06.833 --> 00:01:11.682
The restored process continues at the instruction where the old one stopped.

18
00:01:11.682 --> 00:01:16.148
The heap, the loaded classes and the JIT's compiled code are all back.

19
00:01:16.933 --> 00:01:20.056
You start the JVM with a checkpoint directory.

20
00:01:20.056 --> 00:01:24.536
After warm-up traffic, jcmd writes the image and ends the process.

21
00:01:24.536 --> 00:01:27.727
Then you restore from it, as often as you like.

22
00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:40.603
In the CRaC project's 2020 tests, Spring Boot's first operation took 3,898 ms after a normal start, and 38 ms restored.

23
00:01:41.433 --> 00:01:43.524
But some state can't be saved.

24
00:01:43.524 --> 00:01:50.215
A TCP connection also lives on the other machine, and the card issuer dropped its side long ago.

25
00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:55.384
So by default, CRaC fails the checkpoint while files or sockets are open.

26
00:01:55.384 --> 00:01:56.749
Close them yourself.

27
00:01:57.533 --> 00:02:00.794
Your code takes part through org.crac's Resource.

28
00:02:00.794 --> 00:02:04.587
The ledger pool registers itself with the global context.

29
00:02:04.767 --> 00:02:08.884
Before the checkpoint, beforeCheckpoint closes every connection.

30
00:02:08.884 --> 00:02:11.521
After a restore, afterRestore reconnects.

31
00:02:12.333 --> 00:02:17.278
The context calls beforeCheckpoint newest first, and afterRestore oldest first.

32
00:02:17.278 --> 00:02:22.160
The Kafka consumer writes through the pool, so it stops first and starts last.
